      <title>✖️ The Hustle This machine can kill up to 10,000 weeds a minute. With lasers. Th</title>
      <link>https://t.me/KissmeBabyFeedTwitter/24418</link>
      <description>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;tg-emoji emoji-id="5355148941878900494"&gt;&lt;i class="emoji" style="background-image:url('//telegram.org/img/emoji/40/E29C96.png')"&gt;&lt;b&gt;✖️&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/tg-emoji&gt; &lt;a href="https://x.com/TheHustle/status/2118027396368773584" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;The Hustle&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;This machine can kill up to 10,000 weeds a minute. With lasers.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;That's the idea behind &lt;a href="https://twitter.com/carbon_robotics" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;@carbon_robotics&lt;/a&gt;' LaserWeeder.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;As it rolls through a field, cameras capture every plant underneath it, and an AI decides what's a crop and what's a weed.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Then a laser burns out the weed's meristem, the growth center that produces its new leaves, stems and roots. As long as those cells survive, the weed keeps growing.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Hitting that spot takes sub-millimeter precision.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The target is tiny, the machine never stops moving, and one wrong shot could kill a crop just centimeters away. So 24 NVIDIA GPUs track every weed and correct each shot in real time.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The whole process, from the first image to the laser hitting the meristem, has to happen in about 100 milliseconds.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Killing weeds with a laser, it turns out, is mostly a problem of aim.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <content:encoded>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;tg-emoji emoji-id="5355148941878900494"&gt;&lt;i class="emoji" style="background-image:url('//telegram.org/img/emoji/40/E29C96.png')"&gt;&lt;b&gt;✖️&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/tg-emoji&gt; &lt;a href="https://x.com/TheHustle/status/2118027396368773584" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;The Hustle&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;This machine can kill up to 10,000 weeds a minute. With lasers.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;That's the idea behind &lt;a href="https://twitter.com/carbon_robotics" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;@carbon_robotics&lt;/a&gt;' LaserWeeder.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;As it rolls through a field, cameras capture every plant underneath it, and an AI decides what's a crop and what's a weed.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Then a laser burns out the weed's meristem, the growth center that produces its new leaves, stems and roots. As long as those cells survive, the weed keeps growing.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Hitting that spot takes sub-millimeter precision.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The target is tiny, the machine never stops moving, and one wrong shot could kill a crop just centimeters away. So 24 NVIDIA GPUs track every weed and correct each shot in real time.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The whole process, from the first image to the laser hitting the meristem, has to happen in about 100 milliseconds.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Killing weeds with a laser, it turns out, is mostly a problem of aim.&lt;/div&gt;</content:encoded>
